{'sentiment score': 0, 'confidence': 0, 'reason': ",“Accrued dividends” refer to the amount of money a company owes its shareholders or other stakeholders for dividend payments that have not yet been made. These payments are usually made quarterly or annually, and can be affected by various factors such as the company’s financial performance and industry trends.\n\nIn this case, the company reports an accrued dividend liability of $163,555, which means that they owe their shareholders or other stakeholders a total of $163,555 in dividend payments. This amount is included in the cash flow statement as a non-cash item, as it represents a future payment rather than a current outflow of cash.\n\nThe company’s financial statements and cash flow statement should be carefully reviewed to understand the underlying reasons for this liability and how it will be addressed in the near future. This may involve analyzing the company’s dividend payout policy, its financial performance over time, and any potential risks or challenges that may impact its ability to make these payments.\n\nInvestors and other stakeholders should also consider the following factors when evaluating the company’s dividend policy and cash flow statement:\n\n1. “Cash flows from operating activities”: This section of the cash flow statement shows the inflows and outflows of cash generated by a company's core business operations. Analyzing this section can help investors understand the company’s ability to generate cash from its main sources of revenue.\n\n2. “Cash flows from investing activities”: This section of the cash flow statement shows the inflows and outflows of cash related to a company's investments in assets such as property, plant, and equipment (PP&E), investments, and other long-term assets. Analyzing this section can help investors understand how a company is using its cash to invest in growth opportunities or reduce debt.\n\n3. “Cash flows from financing activities”: This section of the cash flow statement shows the inflows and outflows of cash related to a company's financing activities such as borrowing, repaying loans, issuing stock, and paying dividends. Analyzing this section can help investors understand how a company is managing its debt levels and accessing capital markets to fund its operations.\n\n4. “Accrued expenses”: This item represents the amount of money a company owes to suppliers, employees, or other parties for goods or services provided during the reporting period but not yet paid. Accrued expenses are typically recorded as an asset on the balance sheet and can be reconciled to the cash flow statement.\n\n5. “Deferred revenue”: This item represents the amount of money a company receives from customers in advance of delivering goods or services. Deferred revenue is typically recorded as a liability on the balance sheet and can be reconciled to the cash flow statement.\n\nIn summary, the company’s financial statements and cash flow statement should be carefully reviewed to understand the underlying reasons for its accrued dividend liability and how it will be addressed in the near future. By analyzing various sections of the cash flow statement and considering relevant factors, investors and other stakeholders can gain a better understanding of the company's ability to generate cash from its main sources of revenue and manage its debt levels.", 'company_name': 'COMPANY CONFORMED NAME:\t\t\tAgEagle Aerial Systems Inc.'}